# OTA reconciliation

Model the downlink as **desired state**, not fire-and-forget commands. The
cloud publishes what the device should run; whenever online, the agent
compares desired vs actual and converges. Under intermittent connectivity
there is no lost-command problem — the agent always reconciles to the latest
desired state on next connection.

## Agent role

| Does | Does not |
|------|----------|
| Receive desired-state pointer | Decide promote vs revert quality |
| Verify signed Deployment Manifest | Switch the active model inside the engine |
| Pull + hash-verify artifact over HTTPS/OCI | Push the model binary through the broker |
| Stage into standby / deliver to engine-watched location | Restart the engine for every model swap when hot-reload is available |
| Observe engine liveness + scrape active version | Own shadow-quality evaluation |
| Report milestones on the data plane | |

## Milestone vocabulary (closed enum)

Use a **`Type`-suffixed enum** (see **`pydantic-schemas.mdc`**) for milestones,
e.g. values: `staged`, `shadow-running`, `promoted`, `reverted`, `failed`.
Do not scatter free strings.

## Shadow vs direct switch

Both modes may be required by product. The **model team / engine** selects
based on slot memory/compute headroom. Transient ~2× for shadow must be
feasible under the hosted slot allocation — if not, refuse or defer rather
than starve a neighbour (**`resource-governance.mdc`**, **`deployment-manifest.mdc`**).

## Flow (verify-first)

```
receive desired-state pointer
  → fetch manifest → VERIFY SIGNATURE
    → compatibility / pre-flight
      → pull artifact → VERIFY HASH
        → stage ........................ report staged
          → hand off to engine ......... report shadow-running (when live)
            → scrape active version .... report promoted | reverted | failed
```

Day-one install with empty `hosted_models` is the **same** reconcile path.

